    {
      "body": "Summary cell fixed in cell `5a6fd169`, applied through the live JupyterLab document. Two bugs, not one.\n\n## Bug 1 \u2014 the warning couldn't fire on a single seed\n\n`abs(mean \u2212 floor) < spread` with `spread = max \u2212 min = 0` is never true. Now `tol = max(spread, PRIOR_TOL)` with `PRIOR_TOL = 0.02`, so it works at n=1 and keeps the old multi-seed behavior. Against your actual run:\n\n```\nWARNING: mean best_val_loss (0.2586) is within 0.0200 of the\nprior-collapse floor (0.2656). These runs most likely\nlearned the training class balance and nothing about chirality.\nConfirm it: if last_val_accuracy above equals 0.6667 (the majority-class score),\nthe model is predicting one class for every input.\n```\n\n## Bug 2 \u2014 the floor was hardcoded, and you're about to invalidate it\n\n`PRIOR_COLLAPSE_VAL_MSE = 0.2656` was only correct for the 8-train/7:1 split. The moment your 45-event catalog lands, that constant becomes wrong and the check silently compares against a stale number \u2014 a worse failure than not firing at all, since it would look like it was working. It's now computed from the catalog on every run:\n\n```\ntrivial-predictor reference (11 catalog events, 8 train / 3 val):\n  training class prior            p = 0.1250\n  val MSE predicting only p         = 0.2656   <-- collapse floor\n  val MSE predicting constant 0     = 0.3333\n  val accuracy of majority class    = 0.6667\n```\n\nSo it'll recompute itself for your new catalog with no edit needed. Verified across four scenarios \u2014 collapsed at n=1, collapsed at n=3, genuinely learning, and worse-than-floor (which now suggests checking the trajectory CSV rather than blaming the task).\n\nTwo notes for the new catalog:\n\n- **A missing `split` column is handled** \u2014 it falls back to using all events for both references and prints a `NOTE`. But the numbers are then meaningless as a train/val reference (my test showed it computing `11 train / 11 val`), so include `split` when you build the 45-event file. `FilamentDataset` already reads it at `filament_dataset.py:81-90`.\n- **The reference uses raw catalog labels**, while the datasets additionally drop events with no Surya frame inside `data.ds_time_tolerance`. Those agree only when every event matches \u2014 they do now (11/11), but with 45 events some may not. The cell prints its own train/val counts so you can compare them against the dataloader cell's `train: N samples | val: M samples`. If they disagree, the floor is slightly off.\n\nNotebook-wide syntax check: clean.\n\n## Where things stand\n\nThe pipeline is working and the instability is genuinely fixed \u2014 stable training, head starting at the trivial solution, `val_loss` in a 0.012-wide band across 5 epochs. What it now shows clearly is that 8 training filaments at 7:1 imbalance support learning the class prior and nothing more. That's a clean negative result rather than a broken run, which is the right footing for the bigger catalog.\n\nStill outstanding whenever you want it: the per-step trajectory reader cell (you'd have seen `p \u2248 0.125` directly instead of back-calculating), and the stale train==val `WARNING` at `config_script.yaml:41-44`. Neither blocks the catalog work.Let me look at the new catalog and work out the floors and runtime before designing the run.",
