      "mentions": [],
      "id": "f1805fd1-88cc-4b0b-b3d8-5a975401958f",
      "body": "Now I'll build the Tier 2 notebook.Here's where things stand, including one problem I found on the way back in.\n\n## First: your config file was empty\n\n`configs/config_script.yaml` was 0 bytes, truncated at 14:15 today \u2014 the JupyterHub container restarted ~19 minutes ago, and a buffered write never landed. I rebuilt it from `HEAD` plus the six edits it had, and verified every value against the assertion output your own notebook saved:\n\n```\nindex       full_fil_data.csv | max_samples None\ntargets     ['fc1', 'fc2', 'attn.qkv', 'attn.proj']\nbatch_size  1 | lr 0.0001 | det 'warn' | epochs 20\nwandb_project = filament_chirality\npenult True | pooling class_token | dropout 0.2 | freeze False | lora True r 8\n```\n\nOne thing needed removing that wasn't in my notes: `HEAD` still had `model.nglo: 1`, which is now a load-time error (`nglo` is derived from `pooling`). Replaced with a comment saying so. `load_filament_config()` parses the file cleanly.\n\n## The runtime estimate, redone from measurements\n\nI found hard timing data I was guessing at before \u2014 `runs/noise_floor_seed42/version_3/`:\n\n- `metrics.csv` created **19:56:56**, last written **20:17:40** \u2192 **1244 s for 5 epochs**\n- 34 sample-passes/epoch (23 train + 11 val), batch_size 1, `deterministic: warn`, L40S\n- **249 s/epoch = 7.3 s per sample-pass**, plus ~70 s fixed startup (1.8 GB checkpoint + LoRA + workers)\n- Observed total: **~22 min**\n\n| epochs | optimizer steps (accum 2) | estimated wall |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2 | 24 | ~9.5 min |\n| 5 | 60 | ~22 min |\n| **10** | **120** | **~43 min** |\n| 20 | 240 | ~84 min |\n\n**Why the model knobs are free.** Your cache is on EFS (`fs-03a4e5d8dfd36f570`, 6.3 TB used). One sample is ~1 GB; 1 GB at a typical ~140 MB/s single-stream EFS read is ~7.2 s \u2014 which matches the measured 7.3 s/sample almost exactly. This run is **I/O-bound, not compute-bound**, so pooling, LoRA rank, head size and learning rate cost nothing in wall time. Epochs \u00d7 samples is the only lever.\n\nTwo corrections to what I'd said before compaction:\n\n- I quoted 8.5 s/sample; measured is 7.3 s, so my earlier 25/49/98 min figures were ~15% high.\n- I was going to warn about fresh 2012 downloads. **Withdraw that** \u2014 the 5-epoch run already pulled the whole catalog, so the next run is warm.\n\nAlso, `epoch=4-step=30.ckpt` proves Lightning ran **6** optimizer steps/epoch, not the 5 the notebook printed \u2014 `opt_steps_per_epoch` uses `//` where Lightning uses `ceil`. Cosmetic, but I'll fix it in the new notebook.\n\n## What the trajectory actually says\n\nThis changes my recommendation. `version_3/metrics.csv` per-step (which also answers your earlier \"how do I see the loss at each excursion\" \u2014 it's all in that file):\n\n- `train_loss` 1.01 \u2192 **0.085** by epoch 3. The head fits the training set fine.\n- `val_loss` 0.2605 \u2192 0.2587 \u2192 0.2816 \u2192 0.2890 \u2192 0.2489, against a prior-collapse floor of **0.2524**. Never meaningfully below it.\n- `val accuracy` **0.4545 in all four epochs after the first** = 5/11 = always-dextral.\n\nTrain loss falling to 0.085 while val sits on the floor is **memorization of 23 samples**, not under-training. So I was wrong pre-compaction to plan on \"the binding constraint is the optimizer-step budget, raise the LR\" \u2014 24 steps were already enough to fit. Raising LR alone would just overfit faster. Your `penultimate_linear_layer: false` instinct is the right diagnosis.\n\n## My one favorite Tier 2 run\n\n**Move the task capacity out of the head and into the backbone.**\n\n| knob | Tier 1 | Tier 2 | why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `penultimate_linear_layer` | true | **false** | drops 1,639,680 of 1,642,241 head params \u2192 head becomes one 1280\u21921 linear (1,281 params). Your idea; removes the memorization capacity. |\n| `pooling` | class_token | **global_average** | the class token is *randomly initialized* and the pretrained backbone never saw it \u2014 it must be learned from 23 samples. Average pooling reads the pretrained representation directly with **zero** new parameters. This is the backbone knob and I think it's the highest-value one. |\n| `dropout` | 0.2 | **0.3** | |\n| LoRA `r` / `alpha` | 8 / 8 | **16 / 32** | scaling is \u03b1/r: 1.0 \u2192 2.0, so adapters actually move in ~120 steps |\n| `learning_rate` | 1e-4 | **3e-4** + cosine w/ 10% warmup | warmup is the fix for the 0.25\u21940.39 oscillation |\n| `accumulate_grad_batches` | 4 | **2** | 12 steps/epoch instead of 6 \u2014 double the step budget at identical wall time |\n| `max_epochs` | 5 | **10** | ~43 min |\n\nTrainable: 3,031,040 adapters + 1,281 head = **3,032,321** \u2014 near-identical to Tier 1's 3,157,761, but 99.96% now sits *inside* the pretrained features instead of 52% on top. That makes the experiment crisp: **does Surya's representation contain chirality that a 1,281-parameter readout can find, if the adapters may reshape it?** Tier 1 couldn't ask that, because a 1.6M-param head memorizes 23 samples regardless of what the backbone says.\n\nVerified against the code before recommending: `from_config` passes both `pooling` and `penultimate_linear_layer` (`finetune_models.py:220-221`); with `pooling != class_token` no `head_cls_token` is built and `discover_head_modules` skips param-free `head_dropout`, so `modules_to_save` will be just `['head_unembed']`.\n\nTwo design points I'd add:\n- **`head_unembed.bias` initialized to the train prior (0.4783)** rather than 0, so the run *starts* at the collapse floor and any drop below it is unambiguous. `HEAD_INIT_SCALE` also needs revisiting \u2014 0.01 was tuned for the class-token path, and averaging 65,536 tokens gives a smaller-magnitude input, so I'll fold a one-batch no-grad probe into the run (on `val_loader`, which is `shuffle=False` with no generator, so it consumes no RNG) that prints initial pred mean/std *before* the 43 minutes start.\n- **`num_workers` 4 \u2192 8.** With no augmentation (`drop_hmi_probability: 0.0`) nothing in `__getitem__` draws randomness, so worker count cannot change results \u2014 only wall time. At 137 MB/s aggregate we're nowhere near 8\u00d7150, so this may be the biggest free speedup available.\n\nOne flag: the 1244 s was measured while **229 unrelated files** (2019\u20132021, absent from your catalog) were downloading into the same cache, so treat \u00b130%. Separately, there are 50 stale `.partial` files worth 28 GB from interrupted downloads \u2014 harmless, deletable.\n\nI was about to write the notebook when I got cut off, so it doesn't exist yet \u2014 nothing has been created. Say go and I'll build it, mirroring `noise_floor_runs.ipynb` cell-for-cell with the overrides applied in-notebook (leaving the YAML on Tier 1 settings so the comparison stays reproducible), plus the `ceil` fix and a trajectory-reader cell. If you'd rather swap `global_average` for `global_max` \u2014 defensible, since a filament is localized, though max-pooling gives a much sparser gradient over 65,536 tokens and variance is your enemy at n=23 \u2014 tell me now and I'll build that instead.",
