            "title": "cd filament_kyle && for CASE in 0.18 0.2495 0.40; do python - \"$CASE\" <<'PY' 2>&1 | sed -n '/simulating/,$p' import json, sys, os, math sys.path.insert(0, '/home/jovyan/surya_workshop') import pandas as pd nb = json.load(open('tier2_backbone_lr_lora.ipynb')) cells = [''.join(c['source']) for c in nb['cells'] if c['cell_type'] == 'code'] g = {'__name__': '__main__'} for idx in (0, 1, 2): exec(cells[idx], g) exec(cells[4], g); exec(cells[5], g) bv = float(sys.argv[1]) print(f\"\\n########## simulating best_val_loss = {bv} ##########\") g['results'] = [{\"seed\": 42, \"epochs\": 10, \"opt_steps\": 120, \"trainable\": 3032321, \"best_val_loss\": bv, \"last_val_loss\": bv, \"last_val_accuracy\": 0.4545, \"last_train_loss\": 0.12}] exec(cells[10], g) # verdict cell PY done",
            "raw_output": "########## simulating best_val_loss = 0.18 ##########\n seed  epochs  opt_steps  trainable  best_val_loss  last_val_loss  last_val_accuracy  last_train_loss\n   42      10        120    3032321       0.180000       0.180000           0.454500         0.120000\n\ntrivial-predictor reference (34 catalog events, 23 train / 11 val):\n  training class prior            p = 0.4783\n  val MSE predicting only p         = 0.2524   <-- collapse floor\n  val accuracy always-dextral       = 0.4545\n  val accuracy of majority class    = 0.5455\n\nTier 1 baseline (1 run(s) in noise_floor_summary.csv): best_val_loss = 0.248884\n  NOTE: one Tier 1 run only \u2014 there is no measured seed spread to compare against.\n\n======================================================================\n  RESULT: best_val_loss 0.1800 is below the collapse floor 0.2524\n  by more than 0.0200. Something beyond the class balance was learned.\n  Confirm it two ways before believing it:\n    1. last_val_accuracy must differ from 0.4545 and 0.5455\n       (a constant predictor can still post a decent MSE).\n    2. re-run this config at seeds 43 and 44. One seed is not a measurement.\n\n  vs Tier 1: 0.180000 - 0.248884 = -0.068884\n======================================================================\n########## simulating best_val_loss = 0.2495 ##########\n seed  epochs  opt_steps  trainable  best_val_loss  last_val_loss  last_val_accuracy  last_train_loss\n   42      10        120    3032321       0.249500       0.249500           0.454500         0.120000\n\ntrivial-predictor reference (34 catalog events, 23 train / 11 val):\n  training class prior            p = 0.4783\n  val MSE predicting only p         = 0.2524   <-- collapse floor\n  val accuracy always-dextral       = 0.4545\n  val accuracy of majority class    = 0.5455\n\nTier 1 baseline (1 run(s) in noise_floor_summary.csv): best_val_loss = 0.248884\n  NOTE: one Tier 1 run only \u2014 there is no measured seed spread to compare against.\n\n======================================================================\n  RESULT: best_val_loss 0.2495 is within 0.0200 of the collapse\n  floor 0.2524 \u2014 the run still learned the class balance\n  and nothing about chirality. Shrinking the head did not help.\n  Check last_val_accuracy: if it equals 0.4545 or 0.5455, prediction is constant.\n\n  vs Tier 1: 0.249500 - 0.248884 = +0.000616\n  Within 0.0200 of Tier 1. The intervention changed nothing measurable \u2014\n  which, given it moved 99.96% of the trainable parameters into the backbone,\n  is itself informative: the bottleneck is not head capacity.\n======================================================================\n########## simulating best_val_loss = 0.4 ##########\n seed  epochs  opt_steps  trainable  best_val_loss  last_val_loss  last_val_accuracy  last_train_loss\n   42      10        120    3032321       0.400000       0.400000           0.454500         0.120000\n\ntrivial-predictor reference (34 catalog events, 23 train / 11 val):\n  training class prior            p = 0.4783\n  val MSE predicting only p         = 0.2524   <-- collapse floor\n  val accuracy always-dextral       = 0.4545\n  val accuracy of majority class    = 0.5455\n\nTier 1 baseline (1 run(s) in noise_floor_summary.csv): best_val_loss = 0.248884\n  NOTE: one Tier 1 run only \u2014 there is no measured seed spread to compare against.\n\n======================================================================\n  RESULT: best_val_loss 0.4000 is WORSE than the collapse floor 0.2524\n  \u2014 worse than predicting one constant. Suspect an unstable run rather than a\n  hard task: check the per-step trajectory above for a diverging lr or a\n  train_loss that never descends.\n\n  vs Tier 1: 0.400000 - 0.248884 = +0.151116\n======================================================================\nShell cwd was reset to /home/jovyan/surya_workshop/workshop_infrastructure/models",
